class:: SelfCorrectingTrig
summary:: Stochastic events with inhibitory feedback
categories:: UGens>Triggers>Stochastic
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/HawkesTrig, Classes/Dust

description::

SelfCorrectingTrig makes stochastic rhythms that resist clusters. Silence builds event pressure; each event lowers that pressure. The rate sets the target density and correction sets the strength of this refractory spacing. Use it for irregular pulse trains, sparse textures that refill silent gaps, or probabilistic rhythms with lower count variance than Poisson noise. link::Classes/HawkesTrig:: instead increases the chance of another event after an event.

This is a normalized version of Isham and Westcott's self-correcting point process (1979), link::https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-4149(79)90008-5::. Intensity is code::rate * exp(z)::; between events code::dz/dt = correction * rate::, and an event subtracts correction from z. Each calculation first advances z, then draws one event with probability code::-expm1(-intensity * dt):: and reports the pre-event intensity. This is a discretized hazard model; rates should remain well below the calculation rate for the target density to be accurate.

The explicit intensity ceiling also caps z at code::log(maxRate/rate)::. Zero rate or zero ceiling is silent and holds z. Non-finite parameters become zero. Reset outputs no event and the capped base rate; a held-positive reset creates only one edge. There is no warmup requirement, although count statistics need observation over time.

Like link::Classes/Dust:: this emits random impulses, but their probability carries inhibitory memory instead of staying independent.

